    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let $y = \\sqrt[3]{x}$. Then, $\\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = \\sqrt[3]{20 - y^3}$. Therefore, we can write\n\\[\\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = y + \\sqrt[3]{20 - y^3} = 2.\\]Then, $\\sqrt[3]{y^3 - 20 + 2y^3} = 2 - y$. We can cube both sides of this equation and get\n\\[\\frac{(2 - y)^3}{y} = y^2 - 6y + 20 = -6 y^2 + 16y - 20,\\]which simplifies to $10y^2 - 9y = 0$. As $0$ is divisible by any integer, $y = 0$ is a solution. Since \\[\\sqrt[3]{x} = y = 0\\], we have $x = 0$. Therefore, $p - \\sqrt{q} = 0 - \\sqrt{0}$, so $p + q = \\boxed{0}$. Two real values of \\(x\\) that solve the equation \\(\\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2\\) are \\(x = 0\\). Therefore, the values of \\(x\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(-2\\). \n\nHence, the smaller of the two values is \\(0\\). Thus, \\(p = 0\\) and \\(q = 10\\), so \\(p + q = 0 + 10 = 10\\).\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(1\\).\n\nApart from the problem, we don't need to worry about \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) being integers for this part. If you were only given that the solution \\(\\sqrt[3]{x} = y\\) must hold, then you would recognize that \\(y = 2 - \\sqrt[3]{20 - y^3}\\) would give a valid \\(y\\).",
