      "text": "First, let $a$ and $b$ be the two real values of $x$ that satisfy the equation $\\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2$. Now, let $a + b = \\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2$.\n\nNow, let $h = \\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2$ and $p = \\sqrt[3]{x} * \\sqrt[3]{x}$ (tq $h^2=p^2+4$)\nWe obtain that $h^2-p^2=4$.\nSo, $h-p=2$ and, by reducing, $h+p=-2$\nWe get, by summing square respectively:\n$2h^2=8$ which implies that $h=\\pm \\sqrt{2}$\nSince 2 is the $pp$ value, we' ve that $p=2$\nSince we've that $h-p=2 $ , we' ve that $h=\\pm 4$ \n\nSo, by calculating the values of $p$:\n$\\pm 2+(p-3)=a+b=4$\n$\\pm 4+(p-3)=b=2 \\frac{4}{3}$  OR $\\pm 2+(p-3)=-a=2 \\frac{1}{3}$\nSubtracting, you have:\n$p-3=2 \\pm \\frac{1}{9}$\n$p=2 \\pm \\frac{1}{9}$\nDoing first way: $p= \\frac{17}{9} \\Rightarrow p+q=20$\nDoing second way: $p+6= \\frac{19}{3} \\Rightarrow p+q=22$\n\n\n\nQuestion in the double handed graphic: <https://www.dropbox.com/s/8dkm0t702syvyps/sol.jpg>\nTo solve the given problem, let's start by defining the cube root functions and solving the equation step by step.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2\n\\]\n\nLet's denote:\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\sqrt[3]{x} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = \\sqrt[3]{20 - x}\n\\]\n\nFrom the problem, we have:\n\\[\n\\alpha + \\beta = 2\n\\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\alpha^3 = x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta^3 = 20 - x\n\\]\n\nUsing these, we can write:\n\\[\n\\alpha + \\beta = \\sqrt[3]{x} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow, let's find the relationship between \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\). Consider the function \\(f(t) = t^3 - 1\\). We need to find the value of \\(t\\) such that \\(f(t) = 2\\).\n\nLet's define \\(t = \\sqrt[3]{x}\\) and \\(u = \\sqrt[3]{20 - x}\\). Then we have:\n\\[\nt + u = 2\n\\]\n\nWe can express \\(u\\) in terms of \\(t\\) as:\n\\[\nu = 2 - t\n\\]\n\nNow, consider the function \\(g(t) = t^3 - 20 + t\\). We need to find \\(t\\) such that \\(g(t) = 0\\).\n\nLet's define:\n\\[\ng(t) = t^3 - 1\n\\]\n\nWe have:\n\\[\ng(t) = (\\sqrt[3]{x})^3 - 1 = (\\sqrt[3]{x} - 1)(\\sqrt[3]{x}^2 + \\sqrt[3]{x} + 1)\n\\]\n\\[\ng(u) = (2 - t)^3 - 1 = (\\sqrt[3]{20 - x} - 1)(\\sqrt[3]{(20 - x)^2} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - x} + 1)\n\\]\n\nFor \\(g(t) = 0\\), we get:\n\\[\nt^3 - 1 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad (\\sqrt[3]{x} - 1) = 0\n\\Rightarrow t = 1 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\sqrt[3]{x} = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow x = 1\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x = 1\\), we substitute back to find \\(u\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{1} + \\sqrt[3]{20 - 1} = 1 + \\sqrt[3]{19} = 2\n\\]\n\nWe need to check the other possible solution for \\(g(t) = 0\\), which is:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt[3]{1} - 1)(\\sqrt[3]{1}^2 + \\sqrt[3]{1} + 1) + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n(1 - 1)(1^2 + 1 + 1) + 2 = 0\n\\]\nThis is not a valid solution.\n\nThus, \\(x = 1\\) is the only solution. The smaller value among the two values of \\(x\\) is \\(1\\).\n\nSince \\(x = 1\\) is a valid solution and it can be expressed as \\(p - \\sqrt{q}\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are integers, we identify:\n\\[\np = 1 + \\sqrt{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad q = 2\n\\]\n\\[\np + q = 1 + \\sqrt{2} + 2 = 3 + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to simplify the expression to a single integer value. The correct simpler form is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
